{
  "id": "ep022",
  "duration": 45.893,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 4.328,
      "text": "An octopus has three beating hearts and bright blue copper-based blood.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Octopus vulgaris Merculiano.jpg",
        "credit": "Comingio Merculiano (1845-1915) in Jatta Giuseppe (1860-1903)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Octopus_vulgaris_Merculiano.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 2.533,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 2.533,
          "end": 4.333,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "depth"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 4.328,
      "end": 11.398,
      "text": "Stepping up from a single heart, this creature lacks bones entirely and can squeeze through gaps the size of a coin.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Enteroctopus dofleini in aquarium rotated.jpg",
        "credit": "User:Bachrach44",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enteroctopus_dofleini_in_aquarium_rotated.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.333,
          "end": 7.1,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 7.1,
          "end": 11.4,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "depth"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 11.398,
      "end": 18.443,
      "text": "Moving up the ladder of complexity, the entire animal possesses around five hundred million total neurons.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "PLoSBiol4.e126.Fig6fNeuron.jpg",
        "credit": "Wei-Chung Allen Lee, Hayden Huang, Guoping Feng, Joshua R. Sanes, Emery N. Brown, Peter T. So, Elly Nedivi",
        "license": "CC BY 2.5",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PLoSBiol4.e126.Fig6fNeuron.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 11.4,
          "end": 18.433,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 18.443,
      "end": 25.363,
      "text": "Climbing to the peak of biological weirdness, two-thirds of those cells live directly inside its eight arms.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Img octopus arm and suckers 057513.jpg",
        "credit": "Henryhartley",
        "license": "CC BY 3.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Img_octopus_arm_and_suckers_057513.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 18.433,
          "end": 25.367,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "depth"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 25.363,
      "end": 32.908,
      "text": "Because each arm contains more neurons than many small mammals, it can taste, touch, and move without the central brain.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Pixabay",
        "title": "Octopus, underwater, sea, coconut octopus, ocean, nature, animal",
        "credit": "mr-sragenesia",
        "license": "Pixabay Content License",
        "url": "https://pixabay.com/videos/id-152482/"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 25.367,
          "end": 32.9,
          "kind": "video"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 32.908,
      "end": 41.458,
      "text": "This decentralized mind lets veined octopuses walk their arms to collect discarded coconut shells for portable shelter.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "iNaturalist",
        "title": "Coconut Octopus (Amphioctopus marginatus)",
        "credit": "(c) Ben Costamagna, some rights reserved (CC BY)",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://www.inaturalist.org/observations/399485295"
      },
